[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering MVVM and MVI Architecture Patterns in Android (Java)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern Android development requires robust architectural patterns to ensure maintainability, testability, and scalability. Two of the most prominent patterns derived from the unidirectional data flow paradigm are MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) and MVI (Model-View-Intent). This tutorial explores both architectures implemented in Java."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVVM separates the UI (Activity or Fragment) from the business logic. The ViewModel holds the UI state and communicates with the Repository layer. LiveData or StateFlow is typically used to expose data reactively to the View."},{"order":5,"type":"heading","text":"MVVM Implementation Example"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of an MVVM ViewModel written in Java using LiveData."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public class UserViewModel extends ViewModel {
    private final MutableLiveData<String> userName = new MutableLiveData<>();
    private final UserRepository repository;

    public UserViewModel(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public LiveData<String> getUserName() {
        return userName;
    }

    public void loadUser(int userId) {
        String name = repository.getNameById(userId);
        userName.setValue(name);
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding MVI (Model-View-Intent)"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"MVI takes reactive programming a step further. It is strictly unidirectional and relies on three main components: Intent (user actions or events), Model (the immutable state of the screen), and View (renders the state and emits intents)."},{"order":10,"type":"heading","text":"MVI Implementation Example"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how you can define an Intent and a State class for an MVI architecture in Java."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Define the Intent
public interface UserIntent {
    class LoadUserAction implements UserIntent {
        private final int userId;
        public LoadUserAction(int userId) { this.userId = userId; }
        public int getUserId() { return userId; }
    }
}

// Define the State
public class UserState {
    private final boolean isLoading;
    private final String userName;
    private final String error;

    public UserState(boolean isLoading, String userName, String error) {
        this.isLoading = isLoading;
        this.userName = userName;
        this.error = error;
    }

    public static UserState idle() {
        return new UserState(false, null, null);
    }

    public static UserState loading() {
        return new UserState(true, null, null);
    }

    public static UserState success(String userName) {
        return new UserState(false, userName, null);
    }

    public static UserState error(String error) {
        return new UserState(false, null, error);
    }

    public boolean isLoading() { return isLoading; }
    public String getUserName() { return userName; }
    public String getError() { return error; }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences: MVVM vs MVI"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"State Management: MVVM uses multiple mutable or immutable LiveData fields. MVI uses a single immutable State object representing the entire screen."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Data Flow: MVVM has a bidirectional binding capability or separate flows. MVI strictly enforces a unidirectional cycle: Intent -> Store -> State -> View."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Debugging: MVI makes debugging easier because state changes are predictable and traceable through explicit user intents."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Both MVVM and MVI are excellent choices for Android development in Java. MVVM is easier to adopt and standard in many codebases, while MVI offers superior predictability and state safety for complex applications."}]
